Expected Outcomes
A running Camel application that watches data/input, parses CSV orders, enriches them from PostgreSQL, routes them by value tier, persists them, and writes confirmation reports to data/output
Clear evidence in logs and the filesystem of successful processing (processed/output files, database rows) versus failed processing (error directory, no database rows)
Practical understanding of the Content Enricher, Content-Based Router, and Dead Letter Channel patterns working together in one route
Troubleshooting
Symptom: Application fails at startup with a connection refused or authentication error to PostgreSQL. Diagnostic hint: confirm the PostgreSQL service is running with sudo systemctl status postgresql and confirm you can connect manually using the exact credentials in database.properties with the psql command from Subtask 1.3.
Symptom: CSV file is picked up but CsvOrderProcessor throws a NumberFormatException. Diagnostic hint: check that the CSV file has no extra blank lines or header mismatches, and that setSkipHeaderRecord(true) matches your file actually containing a header row.
Conclusion
In this lab, you built a working Apache Camel integration that reads CSV order files from a filesystem directory, enriches them with customer data from a PostgreSQL database through a HikariCP-backed JDBC connection, routes orders by value tier using a content-based router, and writes confirmation reports while persisting processed orders back to the database. You also configured and verified a dead letter channel so that orders referencing invalid data are retried and then quarantined in an error directory rather than silently failing. Through the input, processed, output, and error directories together with direct database queries, you confirmed both the successful and failure paths of the integration end to end. From here, you can extend the lab by adding a REST-based inventory check step, introducing a message filter for duplicate order numbers, or experimenting with different redelivery and backoff settings on the dead letter channel to observe their effect on failure handling.
